<section id="sec-mistakes" class="col">
  <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
  <ul class="tight">
    <li><strong>The floor’s force kept under the middle.</strong> The upward force is drawn under the centre of the block whatever else acts on it. With the force there, nothing can balance the moment of the push. You catch it by taking moments about the middle of the base. If a push is left with nothing against it, the upward force is in the wrong place.</li>
    <li><strong>The whole width used for the weight.</strong> The stabilising moment is written as \(W\,b\), because the edge \(A\) is the width \(b\) from the far edge. The weight acts on the centre line, which is only half the width from \(A\). You catch it by drawing the line of the weight and measuring level from \(A\) to that line.</li>
    <li><strong>Friction set to its limit while the block stands.</strong> The friction force is taken as \(\mu_s N\) from the start. That is only the most the floor can give. While the block stands, the friction is whatever the push needs, which is \(F = P\). You catch it by writing the sideways balance first and comparing with the limit afterward.</li>
    <li><strong>Only one of the two checks made.</strong> The block is checked against tipping and found safe, and sliding is forgotten, or the other way round. You catch it by always finding both pushes and taking the smaller one.</li>
    <li><strong>The height of the push measured from the wrong level.</strong> The lever arm of the wind is taken from the top of the foundation, where the mast starts, although the block turns about the edge \(A\) of its underside. The moment comes out too small and the block looks safer than it is. You catch it by marking \(A\) on the drawing first and measuring every distance from there.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

